FIX: checkInvalidations potential infinite loop

LOCATION: View.java:1021-1034

PROBLEM:
checkInvalidations uses a Deque and adds to it inside the loop when invalidate() succeeds. Could theoretically loop forever if accusations form a cycle or if invalidate() keeps triggering new additions.

CURRENT CODE PATTERN:
Deque check = new ArrayDeque();
check.add(m);
while (!check.isEmpty()) {
    Participant checked = check.pop();
    context.rings().forEach(ring -> {
        for (Participant q : ring.successors(checked, member -> !member.isAccused())) {
            if (q.isAccusedOn(ring.getIndex())) {
                invalidate(q, ring, check);  // May add to check
            }
        }
    });
}

FIX APPROACH:
1. Add Set visited to track processed members
2. Add iteration limit = 2 * cardinality * rings (theoretical max)
3. Log warning if limit reached (indicates bug or attack)

VALIDATION:
- checkInvalidations terminates for all input patterns
- Limit logging triggers appropriately in test
- Normal accusation chains process correctly

