## Summary
Current pattern creates new virtual thread per round via recursive call. If rounds complete faster than duration, unbounded thread creation possible.

## Location
`memberships/src/main/java/com/hellblazer/delos/membership/messaging/rbc/ReliableBroadcaster.java:300-302`

## Current Code (ANTI-PATTERN)
```java
private void schedule(final Duration duration, ScheduledExecutorService scheduler) {
    Thread.ofVirtual().start(Utils.wrapped(() -> oneRound(duration, scheduler), log));
}

private void oneRound(...) {
    // ... work
    schedule(duration, scheduler);  // Recursive call creates new thread
}
```

## Fix
Use scheduleAtFixedRate for predictable scheduling:
```java
public void start(Duration duration, ...) {
    scheduler = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(1, Thread.ofVirtual().factory());
    scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(
        () -> Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -> oneRound(duration)),
        duration.toMillis(),
        duration.toMillis(),
        TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS
    );
}
```

## Impact
- Unpredictable thread creation
- Potential resource exhaustion under fast rounds
- Harder to reason about timing

## Verification
- Add test validating fixed-rate scheduling
- Verify no thread accumulation over time

