## Summary
The dup() method uses non-atomic read-check-update pattern. Between READ and UPDATE, another thread can modify state, causing race conditions and duplicate message delivery.

## Location
`memberships/src/main/java/com/hellblazer/delos/membership/messaging/rbc/ReliableBroadcaster.java:520-531`

## Current Code (RACE CONDITION)
```java
var previous = state.get(s.hash);  // READ
if (previous != null) {
    int nextAge = Math.max(previous.msg().getAge(), s.msg.getAge());  // CHECK
    if (nextAge > maxAge) {
        state.remove(s.hash);  // UPDATE - race with tick()/gc()
    } else if (previous.msg.getAge() != nextAge) {
        previous.msg().setAge(nextAge);  // UPDATE - concurrent modification
    }
    return true;
}
```

## Fix
Use atomic compute():
```java
AtomicBoolean isDuplicate = new AtomicBoolean(false);
state.compute(s.hash, (k, previous) -> {
    if (previous == null) return null;
    isDuplicate.set(true);
    int nextAge = Math.max(previous.msg().getAge(), s.msg.getAge());
    if (nextAge > maxAge) return null;
    if (previous.msg.getAge() != nextAge) previous.msg().setAge(nextAge);
    return previous;
});
return isDuplicate.get();
```

## Impact
- Duplicate messages delivered to handlers
- Wasted CPU on signature verification
- Violates at-most-once delivery semantics

## Related
- Delos-pqqg (hash derivation) - both affect deduplication
- Thread-unsafe AgedMessage.Builder issue

## Verification
- Add concurrency test validating dup() under contention
- Stress test with parallel receive() calls

