## Summary
ScheduledExecutorService created in start() is never shut down in stop(). Threads continue running after broadcaster stops.

## Location
`memberships/src/main/java/com/hellblazer/delos/membership/messaging/rbc/ReliableBroadcaster.java:196`

## Current Code (LEAK)
```java
public void start(Duration duration, ...) {
    schedule(duration, Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(1, Thread.ofVirtual().factory()));
    // scheduler never stored, never shut down
}

public void stop() {
    // No scheduler.shutdown()
}
```

## Fix
```java
private ScheduledExecutorService scheduler;

public void start(Duration duration, ...) {
    scheduler = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(1, Thread.ofVirtual().factory());
    schedule(duration, scheduler);
}

public void stop() {
    if (scheduler != null) {
        scheduler.shutdown();
        try {
            if (!scheduler.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                scheduler.shutdownNow();
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            scheduler.shutdownNow();
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }
    // ... rest of stop()
}
```

## Impact
- Thread leak accumulates over restarts
- Prevents clean shutdown
- Resource exhaustion in long-running systems

## Verification
- Add test that validates scheduler stops when stop() called
- Verify no threads remain after shutdown

